`_rm_titles` in `libs/core/langchain_core/utils/function_calling.py` only recurses into dict values when stripping `title` keys from a JSON schema. It skips list values entirely, so any schema nested under a JSON Schema list keyword (`anyOf`, `oneOf`, `allOf`) is left completely untouched, titles included. Since Pydantic v2 renders `Optional[SomeModel]` (and any `Union` of a model with `None`) as an `anyOf`, this means `rm_titles=True` behaves inconsistently depending on whether a nested model field is required or optional.

### Affected code

`libs/core/langchain_core/utils/function_calling.py`, function `_rm_titles`:
```python
def _rm_titles(kv: dict[str, Any], prev_key: str = "") -> dict[str, Any]:
    new_kv = {}
    for k, v in kv.items():
        if k == "title":
            if isinstance(v, dict) and prev_key == "properties":
                new_kv[k] = _rm_titles(v, k)
            else:
                continue
        elif isinstance(v, dict):
            new_kv[k] = _rm_titles(v, k)
        else:
            new_kv[k] = v
    return new_kv
```

The `elif isinstance(v, dict)` branch is the only recursion path. When `v` is a `list` (as `anyOf`/`oneOf`/`allOf` always are), it falls into the final `else` and is copied verbatim, titles and all. This function is called by `_convert_json_schema_to_openai_function`, which is the code path behind `convert_to_openai_function` / `convert_to_openai_tool` with the default `rm_titles=True`, i.e. every `model.bind_tools(...)` call using a Pydantic schema.

### Reproduction

Minimal, directly against `_rm_titles`:
```python
from langchain_core.utils.function_calling import _rm_titles

schema = {
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
        "address": {
            "anyOf": [
                {
                    "title": "Address",
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "street": {"title": "Street", "type": "string"},
                    },
                },
                {"type": "null"},
            ],
        }
    },
}

result = _rm_titles(schema)
import json
print(json.dumps(result, indent=2))
```

Output (titles survive inside `anyOf`):
```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "address": {
      "anyOf": [
        {
          "title": "Address",
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {
            "street": {
              "title": "Street",
              "type": "string"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "null"
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
```

Real-world path, showing the required-vs-optional inconsistency with `convert_to_openai_function`:
```python
import json
from pydantic import BaseModel
from langchain_core.utils.function_calling import convert_to_openai_function

class Address(BaseModel):
    """A mailing address."""
    street: str
    city: str

class PersonRequired(BaseModel):
    """A person with a required address."""
    name: str
    address: Address

class PersonOptional(BaseModel):
    """A person with an optional address."""
    name: str
    address: Address | None = None

print(json.dumps(convert_to_openai_function(PersonRequired)["parameters"], indent=2))
print(json.dumps(convert_to_openai_function(PersonOptional)["parameters"], indent=2))
```

`PersonRequired` output has every `title` stripped, as expected:
```json
{
  "properties": {
    "name": {"type": "string"},
    "address": {
      "description": "A mailing address.",
      "properties": {
        "street": {"type": "string"},
        "city": {"type": "string"}
      },
      "required": ["street", "city"],
      "type": "object"
    }
  },
  "required": ["name", "address"]
}
```

`PersonOptional` output (only difference is `Address | None` instead of `Address`) keeps every `title` under the `anyOf` branch:
```json
{
  "properties": {
    "name": {"type": "string"},
    "address": {
      "anyOf": [
        {
          "description": "A mailing address.",
          "properties": {
            "street": {"title": "Street", "type": "string"},
            "city": {"title": "City", "type": "string"}
          },
          "required": ["street", "city"],
          "title": "Address",
          "type": "object"
        },
        {"type": "null"}
      ],
      "default": null
    }
  },
  "required": ["name"]
}
```

Ran against a fresh clone of `main` (`libs/core`, editable install), Python 3.12.10, pydantic 2.x resolved by `uv`/`pip` from the package's own `pyproject.toml`.

### Actual vs expected

Actual: whether `title` is present in the final tool schema sent to the model depends on whether the nested model field happens to be `Optional`/a `Union`, purely because of how Pydantic v2 shapes `anyOf` for those fields. `rm_titles` (the default) silently does nothing for that entire subtree.

Expected: `rm_titles=True` strips `title` consistently regardless of whether the nested schema is reached through a plain `properties`/`items` dict or through an `anyOf`/`oneOf`/`allOf` list. The existing unit tests in `libs/core/tests/unit_tests/utils/test_rm_titles.py` only exercise `properties` and `items` nesting; none of the five parametrized cases include a list-valued key, so this gap wasn't caught.

### Suggested fix direction

`_rm_titles` needs a branch for `isinstance(v, list)` that maps `_rm_titles` over dict elements of the list (preserving non-dict elements as-is), in addition to the existing dict branch.

Not filing a PR for this one, flagging as an issue first since I want to double check there isn't a reason `anyOf`/`oneOf` branches were deliberately left untouched (e.g. some provider relying on titles surviving there) before touching a fairly central schema-conversion helper.

